@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# Cross-node KV cache benchmarks
+
+## Versions this was measured on
+
+| | version | stock? |
+|---|---|---|
+| vLLM | 0.24.0 | **stock** -- installed from PyPI, unmodified |
+| LMCache | 0.4.4 | **stock** on disk; `lmcache-t3knic` monkey-patches it at import (LMCache assumes `torch.cuda`, and the TT path has no CUDA runtime) |
+| vllm-tt-plugin | revision and target tree pinned by matching `kvcache-manager/third_party/vllm-tt-plugin` manifest | **patched** -- 4 files: `worker.py`, `model_runner.py`, `lane_scheduler.py`, `examples/offline_inference_tt.py` |
+| tt-metal | base revision and prepared target tree pinned by the matching `kvcache-manager/third_party/tt-metal` manifest | **patched** -- carries the product-operation return route, multi-shard dual-link transport, gather/scatter updates, and two Moreh ops |
+| kvcache-manager | matching release-candidate commit | **product package** -- owns `Client`, `Server`, host backing, and lifecycle |
+| Python | 3.10 | |
+| Model | Qwen3-32B (HF weights), TP=8, bfp8_b KV | |
+
+The matching kvcache-manager checkout pins the TT-Metal, plugin, vLLM, and LMCache versions and
+provides the preparation and Galaxy environment setup scripts.
+
+The KV path needs the TT-Metal tree prepared by the matching kvcache-manager manifest. The rest of
+the stack is upstream: vLLM is untouched, LMCache's own package is untouched, and the plugin carries
+a patch rather than a fork.
+
+## `run_cross_node_ttft.sh` -- the whole thing, one command
+
+Starts the KV server on the T3K, starts vLLM here, sends the prompt, prints the numbers, and puts
+both sides back down.
+
+```bash
+PLUGIN=YOUR_PATCHED_VLLM_TT_PLUGIN_PATH \
+PLUGIN_PY=YOUR_VLLM_TT_PLUGIN_VENV_PYTHON \
+T3K_HOST=YOUR_T3K_SSH_ALIAS \
+MODEL=YOUR_MODEL_PATH \
+LINK=YOUR_LINK_INDEX \
+    ./run_cross_node_ttft.sh
+```
+
+It expects the cable to be trained already and a compatible config file at `$SHARED/lmcache.yaml`.
+Follow `docs/usage.md` in the matching kvcache-manager checkout for environment and link setup.
+Everything else -- start-gate ordering between the two hosts,
+ready-marker hand-off, the environment both sides must agree on, teardown that does not strand the
+T3K's device lock -- it handles. `PLUGIN` and `PLUGIN_PY` must point to the external checkout and
+environment created from the matching kvcache-manager manifest. Override `TT_REPO`, `T3K_REPO`,
+`SHARED`, `LINK`, `PORT`, `REQUESTS`, `OSL` as needed.
+
+Use `t3knic_ttft.py` below directly when a server is already running and you just want to re-measure.
+
+## `t3knic_ttft.py`
+
+Sends one cold request and at least two warm requests against a running vLLM server and reports
+TTFT for each. The first request misses and stores its KV; later requests restore it across the
+cable, so the gap is what the cross-node cache buys.
+
+```bash
+python3 t3knic_ttft.py                  # ISL 3296 / OSL 16, 3 requests
+python3 t3knic_ttft.py --requests 5     # more warm samples
+```
+
+Three requests, not two, because the check is that the warm answers agree with **each other**: one
+warm request cannot disagree with anything. See "What counts as correct" below.
+
+It measures requests only -- no device reset, no link retrain, no lock handling. Bring the server
+up however the deployment does, then run this against it.
+
+### What counts as correct
+
+A warm answer is **not** required to match the cold one, and the script does not fail when it does
+not. Cold prefills the whole prompt in one pass; warm restores whole blocks and forwards only the
+ragged tail. The two reduce in a different order, so their arithmetic can differ far enough into a
+greedy decode to flip a token -- on the shipped prompt it flips token 14 of 16, and on a different
+prompt of the same length it does not flip at all.
+
+What **is** required is that the warm answers agree with each other: they all take the same path.
+The script fails on that. Neither check proves the restored KV equals the stored KV -- run with
+`T3KNIC_KVDIFF=1` when that is the question; it digests the KV at both boundaries and compares.
+
+One more thing to get right or the number is meaningless:
+
+* **vLLM's own prefix caching must be off** (`--no-enable-prefix-caching`). Otherwise the second
+  request hits vLLM's cache and never reaches the connector, and the result says nothing about the
+  cross-node path.
+
+The script prints the engine's `prompt_tokens`, so a run proves what ISL it actually sent rather
+than trusting the prompt file.
+
+### Reference result
+
+The matching `kvcache-manager` README is the single source for the validated TTFT result and
+methodology. Do not duplicate environment-specific measurements here.
+
+### KV cache size
+
+Qwen3-32B keeps 64 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 head_dim in bfp8_b, sharded one KV head per chip at
+TP=8. A bfp8 tile carries 64 B of shared exponents per 1024 elements, so an element costs 17/16 B.
+
+| | size |
+|---|---|
+| per token | 139,264 B = **136 KiB** (17 KiB per chip) |
+| per block (128 tokens) | **17.00 MiB** (2.125 MiB per chip) |
+| **ISL 3296** | 437.75 MiB of real tokens, stored as 26 blocks = **442.00 MiB** (55.25 MiB per chip) |
+| whole cache at `num_gpu_blocks=1025` | 17.02 GiB (2.13 GiB per chip) |
+
+The 442 MiB is what crosses the cable: LMCache stores whole 128-token chunks, so 3296 tokens round
+up to 26 of them.
